The Nautilus DevOps team is automating IAM user creation using Terraform for better identity management.

For this task, create an AWS IAM User using Terraform with the following requirements:

The IAM User name iamuser_anita should be stored in a variable named KKE_user.
Note:

1. The configuration values should be stored in a variables.tf file.

2. The Terraform script should be structured with a main.tf file referencing variables.tf.
The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To satisfy this task, you need two files: variables.tf (holding the IAM user name) and main.tf (referencing that variable). The IAM user name must be stored in a variable named exactly KKE_user.

📄 /home/bob/terraform/variables.tf
hcl
variable "KKE_user" {
  description = "Name of the IAM user"
  type        = string
  default     = "iamuser_anita"
}
📄 /home/bob/terraform/main.tf
hcl
resource "aws_iam_user" "iamuser_anita" {
  name = var.KKE_user

  tags = {
    Name = var.KKE_user
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Variable KKE_user: Holds the IAM user name iamuser_anita, exactly as required.

name argument: var.KKE_user is passed directly to the IAM user's name field.

Name tag: Added for clarity and consistency with the variable.

No hardcoded values in main.tf: All configuration lives in variables.tf, keeping the script properly structured.

Region: IAM is global, so no region-specific configuration is needed (your provider.tf already handles this).

🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Confirm No Changes
bash
terraform plan
It should return:

text
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
🔍 Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 iam get-user --user-name iamuser_anita
You should see the user iamuser_anita returned with its ARN and creation date, confirming the variable was applied correctly.
